WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:11.190
Hello and welcome to The English We Speak

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.180
with me, Feifei…

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.860
…and me, Roy.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.020
In this programme, we have a word that is

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.830
used to say that something is amazing or extremely

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.530
good. Speaking of which have you seen that

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.480
new blockbuster that everyone’s talking

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.020
about, ‘Unicorns Eat Bananas in the Stars’?

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.430
What kind of title is that?! That film

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.510
sounds awful!

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.930
It’s not! ‘Unicorns Eat Bananas in the

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.550
Stars’ is fire!

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.060
Fire? The unicorns that eat bananas in the

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.690
stars are on fire?! What is this film about?

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.440
No, Roy! Don’t be silly, the banana eating

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.490
unicorns aren’t on fire! That would be ridiculous.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:57.330
I said the film is fire. I mean that the film

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.260
is amazing!

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.440
Ahhh of course! We use 'fire' to describe

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.110
something that is really good! For example,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.780
your T-shirt is fire, Feifei!

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.330
Thanks, Roy! I bought it this week! It’s

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.340
official merchandise for the film!

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.250
Anyway, let’s listen to these examples…

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:20.570
Have you checked out my new phone? It’s

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.780
fire. It can do so many cool things!

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.800
I went to a concert to see ‘Rob’s Rolling

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.440
Biscuits’ last night. Their new song is

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:30.920
fire.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.520
That new series everyone is talking about

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.760
is fire! It’s got dragons, zombies and

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.680
vampires in it. I love it!

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:47.570
This is The English We Speak from BBC Learning

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.700
English and we’re talking about the word

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.760
‘fire’, which is used to say that something

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.110
is amazing.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.360
I might have to go tonight and watch the film

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.080
at the cinema.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.440
You definitely should. It’s straight fire!

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.140
We sometimes put the word ‘straight’ in

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.470
front of ‘fire’. Straight here means ‘very’.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.560
So I probably should have said your t-shirt

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.000
is straight fire, then.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.910
Only if that’s what you really think! You

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.140
haven’t even seen the film yet!

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.990
What about my T-shirt? Do you think it’s

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:18.200
fire?

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.560
Honestly, no. It looks like it needs to be

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.530
put in a fire. You should just burn it. The

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.290
film on your T-shirt is not fire! That film

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:28.520
is terrible.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.460
OK, maybe I’ll buy a new one today.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.210
Bye, Roy.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:02:59.240
Bye.

